<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joan, Countess of Toulouse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1220">1220</a>–<a href="/years/1271">1271</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Joan of Toulouse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joan was the only child and heiress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vii-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VII, Count of Toulouse</a>. Under the Treaty of Paris of 1229, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, she was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-count-of-poitiers">Alphonse of Poitiers</a>, brother of Louis IX. When she and Alphonse died childless within days of each other in 1271, the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> passed to the French crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-countess-of-toulouse.jpg" alt="Joan, Countess of Toulouse" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cartes Postales Labouche · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeanne_de_Toulouse.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joan was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vii-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VII, Count of Toulouse</a>, by his first wife, Sancha of Aragon. In 1225, at the age of five, she was betrothed to Hugh, eldest son and heir of Hugh X of Lusignan and of Isabella, Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a> and dowager queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, but the engagement was soon broken.</p><p>The Treaty of Paris, signed on 12 April 1229, required her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-count-of-poitiers">Alphonse, Count of Poitiers</a>, and a papal dispensation for their kinship in the fourth degree is dated 26 June of that year. She was then brought up at the French royal court. The date of the marriage itself is uncertain; both 1234 and 1241 have been suggested, the earlier being thought more likely.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Joan accompanied her husband on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade">Seventh Crusade</a> in 1249. Her father died that year, and she succeeded him as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, with Alphonse as co-ruler. While they were away, her mother-in-law installed a governor for them. The couple took control of their lands in October 1250 and made their formal entry as countess and count of Toulouse in May 1251, after which they confirmed the governor and left again; they seldom visited the county. She went with Alphonse on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighth-crusade">Eighth Crusade</a> in 1270.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Joan died on 25 August 1271, four days after Alphonse. In her will of 23 June 1270 she named as her universal heiress Philippa de Lomagne, her closest relative, a great-granddaughter of Constance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, half-sister of Raymond VII. The Parlement invalidated the will in 1274. One bequest in Alphonse's will was allowed: the lands of the Comtat Venaissin passed to the Holy See, which held them as papal territory until 1791.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joan of Toulouse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The intervention of the French crown proved decisive: Louis VIII’s crusade of 1226 broke resistance, and by the Treaty of Paris of 1229 Raymond VII kept a reduced county for life, while his only daughter <b>Joan</b> was married to Alphonse of Poitiers, brother of Louis IX.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Llywelyn issued charters, kept a household of officials led by his steward, the distain Ednyfed Fychan, married <b>Joan</b>, daughter of King John of England, and by the Treaty of Worcester of 1218 obtained recognition of his conquests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Siena, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.6,1.44&amp;year=1253" aria-label="Toulouse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4585 618 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joan, Countess of Toulouse"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5035 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5035 888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.6,1.44&amp;year=1253">What was here in 1253? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
